205
00:08:38,640 --> 00:08:39,599
things that we're going to be talking

206
00:08:39,599 --> 00:08:42,240
about. Replay Attacks are

207
00:08:42,240 --> 00:08:44,320
fundamental threats in any authenticated

208
00:08:44,320 --> 00:08:47,519
messaging system. The concept is that

209
00:08:47,519 --> 00:08:49,760
an attacker will intercept a message

210
00:08:49,760 --> 00:08:51,839
that was legitimately created and signed

211
00:08:51,839 --> 00:08:54,399
and then is able to resend that

212
00:08:54,399 --> 00:08:56,880
replaying it to the receiver at a

213
00:08:56,880 --> 00:08:59,120
later time. If the receiver has no

214
00:08:59,120 --> 00:09:02,160
mechanism to detect that the message is

215
00:09:02,160 --> 00:09:04,080
a duplicate, it may process it again as

216
00:09:04,080 --> 00:09:06,320
if it were fresh. And depending on

217
00:09:06,320 --> 00:09:07,680
the message type, this could grant

218
00:09:07,680 --> 00:09:09,680
unauthorized access, trigger

219
00:09:09,680 --> 00:09:12,880
duplicate operations, or otherwise

220
00:09:12,880 --> 00:09:15,360
cause harm. It's important to note

221
00:09:15,360 --> 00:09:17,120
that the attacker doesn't need to forge

222
00:09:17,120 --> 00:09:19,279
or modify anything in the case of a

223
00:09:19,279 --> 00:09:21,680
replay attack. The message is

224
00:09:21,680 --> 00:09:23,839
genuinely signed by the real sender and

225
00:09:23,839 --> 00:09:26,080
the attacker can exploit the

226
00:09:26,080 --> 00:09:27,360
fact that message has a valid

227
00:09:27,360 --> 00:09:29,920
signature on it. But that doesn't

228
00:09:29,920 --> 00:09:32,080
mean that the message is provably

229
00:09:32,080 --> 00:09:34,959
delivered right now.

230
00:09:34,959 --> 00:09:38,080
This is why timeliness is a

231
00:09:38,080 --> 00:09:40,480
core mechanic for KRAM. A message could

232
00:09:40,480 --> 00:09:44,000
be properly signed but it must also

233
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:46,800
fall within the timeliness window that

234
00:09:46,800 --> 00:09:52,000
is able to correctly filter out

235
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,880
any messages that may

236
00:09:54,880 --> 00:09:59,200
be sent by malicious bad actors.

237
00:09:59,200 --> 00:10:01,920
– In current-use protocols, how is that

238
00:10:01,920 --> 00:10:03,845
